{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"weary-wavy-hen","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Rename the three bridge verbs that SURVIVE the redesign onto the `bridge` noun, migrating every\nenumerated consumer of each:\n\n* `rebar bridge-fsck`   -> `rebar bridge fsck`\n* `rebar bridge-probe`  -> `rebar bridge check-access`\n* `rebar jira-onboard`  -> `rebar bridge setup`\n\nCarved out of `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a`, which now owns only the `bridge` verb-group registration.\n`depends_on 4752` because the noun and its dispatch must exist before verbs can attach to it.\n\n## These are RENAMES, not new features — this is what the reviews caught\n\n`check-access` is NOT new work. `bridge-probe` already ships the identical create/label/search/\ndelete Jira round-trip (`src/rebar/_engine/jira-capability-probe.py`, launched via\n`_cli/__init__.py:163`'s `_bridge_probe`), so this is a rename plus consumer migration, not an\nimplementation. `bridge setup` is likewise the EXISTING `jira-onboard` wizard renamed\n(`src/rebar/_cli/_jira_onboard.py`), which already implements persist-config-then-probe. Scoping\nthese as renames is the whole point of the split: the work is enumerating and migrating consumers,\nand each rename's consumer set is listed below rather than left implicit.\n\n## Why `check-access` stays its own verb rather than becoming a flag (carried from `4752`)\n\nAll three narrowing-flag precedents — `flux check --pre`, `dbt debug --connection`,\n`gh auth status` — are READ-ONLY, and none mutates the remote. The precedent therefore does not\ntransfer to an operation that CREATES AND DELETES A REAL JIRA ISSUE. `fsck` audits your records;\n`check-access` proves your access. They are different questions with different blast radii.\n\n`setup` calls `check-access` as its final validation step (this is already how `jira-onboard`\nends, via `_jira_onboard.py:196-200` invoking `_bridge_probe` with the just-persisted settings\ninjected as an env overlay; the rename must preserve that overlay behaviour and the\n`--no-validate` escape hatch).\n\n## Enumerated consumers — `bridge-fsck` -> `bridge fsck`\n\nCLI: `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py` (`_BRIDGE` frozenset ~121, dispatch arm ~309),\n`src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge-fsck.txt`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt`.\nSchema: `src/rebar/schemas/bridge_fsck.schema.json` — BOTH the `$id`\n(`.../schemas/bridge_fsck.schema.json`) and the `description`, which names\n`rebar bridge-fsck --output json`.\nScripts/CI: `scripts/canary_bridge.py:412` (`runner([\"rebar\",\"bridge-fsck\",\"--output\",\"json\"])`)\nplus its ticket-body strings at 449/464/466/497/531; `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`\n(header comment :8, step comment :186); `scripts/probe_rebar.py:505,515,516`.\nTests: `tests/unit/test_canary_bridge_wiring.py:78` (asserts the literal `\"rebar bridge-fsck\"`),\n`tests/scripts/test_canary_bridge.py` (FakeRunner keys `(\"rebar\",\"bridge-fsck\")` and the drift\ntitle assertion at ~510), `tests/scripts/test_canary_bridge_heldout.py`,\n`tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck.py`,\n`tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck_binding_drift.py`,\n`tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck_keyset_prev_heldout.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/conftest.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/contracts/test_schema_outputs.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/contracts/test_schema_coverage.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_output_schema_coverage.py`.\nDocs: `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`, `docs/exit-codes.md:78`,\n`docs/output-schemas.md:50`, `docs/mcp-reference.md`, `docs/architecture.md`,\n`docs/event-schema.md`, `docs/maintenance-audit-runbook.md`, `docs/adr/0027-reconciler-binding-lifecycle.md`.\nOUT OF SCOPE: the LIBRARY/MCP `bridge_fsck` symbol names (`src/rebar/__init__.py`,\n`src/rebar/_lib_ops.py:84`, `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py`,\n`src/rebar/types.py`) belong to sibling `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d`, which owns the library and MCP\nbridge surface. This ticket renames the CLI verb and updates the shared JSON-schema text only.\n\n## Enumerated consumers — `bridge-probe` -> `bridge check-access`\n\nCLI: `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py:163` (`_bridge_probe` launcher + its docstring) and the dispatch\narm at `393-394`; `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge-probe.txt` (renamed);\n`src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt:31`.\nImplementation docstring: `src/rebar/_engine/jira-capability-probe.py:7` (\"Run it as a preflight\nbefore relying on Jira sync (`rebar bridge-probe`)\").\nIn-tree caller: `src/rebar/_cli/_jira_onboard.py:14,94,102,182,186,191,196-200` — the wizard's\nhelp text, its `--no-validate` help string, its two user-facing \"run `rebar bridge-probe`\" hints,\nand its actual `_bridge_probe(...)` call.\nTests: `tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py` — asserts the literal string `\"bridge-probe\"` in wizard\noutput (239, 266, 274) AND monkeypatches the symbol BY NAME at 251, 268, 280\n(`monkeypatch.setattr(\"rebar._cli._bridge_probe\", ...)`), so a symbol rename must be grepped for as\na string, not just resolved by the LSP;\n`tests/interfaces/contracts/test_help_overview_coverage.py:59` (verb inventory).\nDocs: `docs/cli-reference.md:30,33`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:54,68,242`,\n`docs/releasing.md:430,433` (the release checklist runs the six-step round-trip).\n\n## Enumerated consumers — `jira-onboard` -> `bridge setup`\n\nCLI: `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py:66` (the intercept-verb list) and the intercept at `562-567`;\n`src/rebar/_cli/_jira_onboard.py:1,87-90` (module docstring, `jira_onboard()` entry, and its\nargparse `prog=\"rebar jira-onboard\"` — the `prog` string is user-facing in `--help`).\nNOTE the established precedent recorded when the wizard shipped (`b5db-7433`): it is an INTERCEPT,\nso argparse owns `--help`, and there is intentionally NO pinned `help/jira-onboard.txt` and no\n`overview.txt` entry. Moving it under the `bridge` noun must either preserve that intercept shape\nor wire overview + routing + a `.txt` together — adding a `.txt` alone BREAKS\n`test_help_overview_coverage`.\nReference generator: `scripts/gen_cli_reference.py:62` (the `\"jira-onboard\"` entry) and its pin\n`tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py:39`.\nTests: `tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py` drives `main([\"jira-onboard\", ...])` at 183, 203, 212,\n228, 236, 252, 270, 281, 294, 305 — every one migrates.\nDocs: `docs/cli-reference.md:479`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:51,58,59,61`, `docs/config.md:259`\n(the https-only note), `docs/adr/0007-jira-onboard-config-write.md` (its title and body name the\nverb; the ADR is amended with a rename note rather than rewritten, since ADRs record history).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge fsck`, `rebar bridge check-access` and `rebar bridge setup` all dispatch, and\n      the old spellings `bridge-fsck`, `bridge-probe`, `jira-onboard` no longer do; none appears in\n      `rebar --help`. No aliases ship (the redesign's no-alias rule)\n- [ ] Each rename is proven to be BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING, not a reimplementation: `bridge\n      check-access` performs the same create/label/search/delete Jira round-trip `bridge-probe` did\n      (same `jira-capability-probe.py` code path), and `bridge setup` runs the same\n      detect/prompt/persist/validate wizard, with `--no-validate`, `--reset`, `--yes`,\n      `--url/--user/--project` all still accepted\n- [ ] `bridge setup` calls `bridge check-access` as its FINAL validation step, still injecting the\n      just-persisted settings as an env overlay, and still skipping the check when\n      `JIRA_API_TOKEN` is unset (exit 0) or `--no-validate` is passed\n- [ ] `check-access` ships as its own verb — there is no `bridge fsck --check-access` flag spelling\n      and no alias; the rationale (all three narrowing-flag precedents are read-only, while this\n      creates and deletes a real Jira issue) is recorded in the shipped help text or docs\n- [ ] `src/rebar/schemas/bridge_fsck.schema.json` has BOTH its `$id` and its `description` updated\n      to the new verb name, and the schema-coverage / schema-output / MCP-schema contract tests pass\n      against the new registration\n- [ ] Every consumer enumerated above is migrated in the same change. In particular the STRING-named\n      sites are covered: `tests/unit/test_jira_onboard.py`'s\n      `monkeypatch.setattr(\"rebar._cli._bridge_probe\", ...)` (3 sites) and\n      `tests/unit/test_canary_bridge_wiring.py:78`'s literal `\"rebar bridge-fsck\"` — a symbol-graph\n      search alone misses these, so the change is validated with a plain grep for each old verb name\n- [ ] A test greps the shipped tree and fails if any user-facing string still names `bridge-fsck`,\n      `bridge-probe` or `jira-onboard`, excluding CHANGELOG/`docs/archive`/ADR history\n- [ ] `scripts/canary_bridge.py` and `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge-canary.yml` invoke the new\n      verb, and the canary still classifies binding drift identically (exit 1 with valid JSON is\n      still DRIFT DATA, not an error)\n- [ ] `scripts/gen_cli_reference.py` + `tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py` are updated together\n      and the regenerated `docs/cli-reference.md` matches\n- [ ] The library and MCP `bridge_fsck` entrypoints are UNCHANGED by this ticket and still callable\n      at its boundary; their rename belongs to `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d`\n- [ ] Release notes record all three renames with their old -> new mapping and the no-alias decision","id":"e27c-5a20-cd13-43b2","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","priority":1,"tags":["bridge","ux"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Rename the three surviving bridge verbs onto the bridge noun"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786095601138647001,"uuid":"0fcf1412-b063-4114-b476-8bc467077cfd"}